<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho VI William of Gascony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1032">1032</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho VI William was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> from about 1009 to 1032, the last male ruler of the native ducal line. He had close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Pamplona</a> and governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>. On his death without sons Gascony passed through his sisters' families, and after a succession struggle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-viii-duke-of-aquitaine">Guy-Geoffrey</a> of Poitou united it with Aquitaine in the 1050s.</p></div>
